From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <opus5@local>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 07:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: fold the MoE expert aggregation into the rms_norm
 that consumes it (+1.62% decode, bit-exact)

The series README prices the last surviving `k_bin_bcast` population, 40 per
token, as the shared-expert add. It is not that. Decoding the template
instantiations in a rocprofv3 kernel trace of the 24-patch frontier splits the
population in two:

    k_bin_bcast<op_add, f32,f32,f32, 7 x const float *>   39/token, 1.58 us
    k_bin_bcast<op_add, f32,f32,f32, 1 x const float *>    1/token, 1.76 us

The trailing pointer pack is the fusion width, so the 39-launch family is
`n_fuse = 7`: the routed-expert aggregation, `moe_out = e0 + e1 + ... + e7`
over the eight channel views of the routed down projection. The shared-expert
add is already free - it is one of the two adds inside
`rms_norm_pre_add_f32<1024, 3, true>` (38/token), which patch 0008 folds into
the following attention norm along with the residual add.

So the MoE block ends in ONE add chain of nine nodes,

    (((e0 + e1) + ... + e7) + ffn_shexp) + ffn_inp  ->  rms_norm -> mul

and it is split across two dispatches for a layout reason, not a graph one.
`ggml_cuda_op_fused_add` applies a SINGLE src1 stride to every operand it
fuses - `k_bin_bcast` computes one `i_src1` and one `s10` for the whole
pointer pack - and the routed results are channel views of one mul_mat_id
output with row stride `n_expert_used*n_embd`, while `ffn_shexp` and
`ffn_inp` are plain contiguous rows. The chain walk therefore stops at that
boundary, emits the first seven adds as their own launch, and leaves a two-add
remainder for the norm fold.

`rms_norm_pre_add_f32` has no such constraint: `rms_norm_pre_add_args` already
carries a `stride_row` per operand. Dropping the same-layout test from the
norm fold's chain walk - and only from it; the generic multi-add fusion keeps
it - lets the whole nine-add chain land in the norm kernel:

  - `RMS_NORM_ADD_MAX_OPERANDS` 8 -> 12, launch switches extended to match
  - the chain walk's cap follows that constant instead of a literal 6
  - an alias guard: pass 1 stores the chain result while reading every operand
    at the SAME element index, so an operand may share the result's base
    pointer (its value at that index is consumed before the store) but must
    not overlap it at a shift. This matters once the chain reaches back into
    the aggregation, because ggml-alloc can hand the chain result a block the
    mul_mat_id output also occupies; the channel views tile that output at
    exactly this tensor's size, so the coincident view is base-equal and every
    other view is disjoint.

Accumulation stays left-to-right from src0 of the first add, which is exactly
what the `k_bin_bcast` comma fold followed by the norm's own chain already
computed, so no intermediate float changes. Decode-shape perplexity
(`-c 512 -b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8`) is identical to every digit and every
chunk: PPL 5.2682, [1]5.2972 [2]5.9571 [3]5.5067 [4]5.5793 [5]4.9936
[6]4.9487 [7]5.1110 [8]5.2682.

Measured alone with llama-bench, same binary, `GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_WIDE_PRE_ADD`
toggle, six whole-process rounds with rotating arm order, median of per-round
ratios: decode 1.0162 (all six rounds 1.0145-1.0201), prefill 0.9985. Census:
739.1 -> 701.1 dispatches/token, total kernel 4.933 -> 4.853 ms/token. The
`k_bin_bcast` population is gone and the norm goes `<1024,3>` at 2.92 us to
`<1024,10>` at 3.18 us: it absorbs 65.8 us/token of launches for 9.7 us of
extra work, plus 38 fewer inter-dispatch gaps at ~1.8 us each.

Prefill is untouched by construction. With n_tokens > 1 the channel views are
not contiguous, the operand test declines them, and the two-add remainder
folds exactly as before.
